{"label":"2B","direction":"supports","claim":"AVITI is the only rival short-read platform with a measurable GEO footprint, and its human sample count roughly doubled in 2026 from a very small base.","detail":"Human 'Element AVITI' platform GPL34295 had 138 GSM samples released in 2024, 1,355 in 2025 (62/446/458/389 by quarter) and 2,195 in Jan–Sep 2026 (365/999/831). Annualizing 2026 gives about 2,930, roughly +116% vs 2025. Mouse AVITI (GPL34475) added 1,753 samples in Jan–Sep 2026. Human plus mouse AVITI is about 3,950 in 2026 YTD, against about 95,400 human NovaSeq 6000 samples in the same period, or about 4%. Rival adoption is real and accelerating, but AVITI is still only 2–3% of human 6000+X volume in a quarter. Samples are not Gb, and GEO release lags the sequencing by months.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL34295[ACCN]+AND+gsm[ETYP]+AND+2026/01/01:2026/09/30[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GEO via E-utilities (GPL34295, GPL34475)","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Earlier GEO work measured only 6000 vs X; this adds a quantified AVITI series (GPL34295/GPL34475)."},
{"label":"2B","direction":"supports","claim":"Per-organism AVITI platform records are proliferating on GEO, showing a broad research user base spanning many species.","detail":"A GEO title search for 'AVITI' returns 64 platform records, almost all one per organism (E. coli/MS2, Brachypodium, Cylindrotheca, Chlorocebus, Pan troglodytes, Octodon degus and others). The latest were created 14 Jul–29 Sep 2026 (GPL37215–GPL37604). New AVITI entries are appearing nearly weekly, so many labs are now submitting AVITI data, though most new records have 0–2 samples.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=AVITI[Title]+AND+gpl[ETYP]&retmax=20","date":"2026-09-29","source_name":"NCBI GEO platform records","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Gives a count (64 AVITI platform records) and creation dates not in the known list."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"The X's share of human 6000+X GEO samples stepped up in Q1'26 and has stalled at about 12.6–12.8% for three quarters, so literature-visible conversion is incomplete.","detail":"X/(6000+X) human GSMs: Q1'25 1,761/29,303=6.0%; Q2'25 7.6%; Q3'25 2,353/32,464=7.2%; Q4'25 2,530/33,385=7.6%; Q1'26 4,333/33,780=12.8%; Q2'26 4,269/33,890=12.6%; Q3'26 5,268/41,580=12.7%. The 6000 stays at about 87% of the combined count. GEO lags sequencing by 1–2 years and covers research data only, so this is a trailing indicator of research conversion, not clinical.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL34281[ACCN]+AND+gsm[ETYP]+AND+2026/07/01:2026/09/30[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GEO (GPL24676, GPL34281)","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Adds full 2025Q1–2026Q3 quarterly series; the known item gave only a Q1→Q2 2026 flatten."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"Q3'26 human NovaSeq 6000 GEO samples jumped to 36,312, a 7-quarter high, so the 6000 tail is not visibly shrinking in published data.","detail":"The 6000 series runs 27,542 (Q1'25), 29,509, 30,111, 30,855, 29,447 (Q1'26), 29,621, then 36,312 (Q3'26, +22.6% q/q). Months in Q3'26: Jul 15,621, Aug 10,617, Sep 10,074. The Q3 rise is not one giant series: 6000 series count rose from 1,408 to 1,647 (+17%), and samples per series were 21.0 vs 22.0. It is a lagged read of 2024–25 6000 sequencing, so it fits the 6000 tail being large.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL24676[ACCN]+AND+gsm[ETYP]+AND+2026/07/01:2026/09/30[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GEO (GPL24676)","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Q3'26 count, monthly split and series-vs-sample check are new."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"against","claim":"GEO shows no collapse in 6000-labelled data and a slow-moving X share, which is consistent with Illumina's 'long tail' narrative rather than rapid tail loss; but equally it hints the tail is research and lagged.","detail":"The 6000's quarterly sample count is up 7.9% over Q1'25→Q1'26 (27,542→29,447) and up 31.8% over Q1'25→Q3'26. The X is still only about 13% of combined samples versus 78–83% X share of Gb shipped, so GEO is a heavily lagged and research-biased view and should not be used to test 2027 clinical mix. The 2024 baseline: 6000 26,287 (Q1'24) and 34,326 (Q3'24), X 36 and 538 (1.5% share in Q3'24).","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL24676[ACCN]+AND+gsm[ETYP]+AND+2024/07/01:2024/09/30[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GEO (GPL24676, GPL34281)","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Quantifies the lag between GEO share (~13%) and shipped-Gb share (~83%) and adds 2024 baselines."},
{"label":"2A","direction":"supports","claim":"X-platform GEO series are growing faster than 6000 series, but each X series carries about as many samples, so newer data is not larger-scale yet.","detail":"Q3'26 vs Q2'26: X series 251 vs 177 (+42%); 6000 series 1,647 vs 1,408 (+17%). X samples per series were about 21 (5,268/251) vs about 24 (4,269/177). X's share of 6000+X series was 13.2% in Q3 vs 11.2% in Q2. The research community is adopting the X, with no sign of bulk-lane megaprojects driving it.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL34281[ACCN]+AND+gse[ETYP]+AND+2026/07/01:2026/09/30[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GEO series counts","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Series-level counts were not in the earlier GEO work."},
{"label":"2B","direction":"supports","claim":"AVITI reached 2.9% of human 6000+X samples in Q2'26 before slipping to 2.0% in Q3'26, so the rival's research share is small and lumpy.","detail":"AVITI human 999 in Q2'26 vs 6000+X 33,890 = 2.9%; Q3'26 831/41,580 = 2.0%; Q4'25 389/33,385 = 1.2%; Q1'25 62/29,303 = 0.2%. The Q2 peak looks like a big batch release. Series for AVITI over Apr–Sep 2026 total only 87. The pattern is up from about 0.2% to about 2–3% in six quarters, but not yet at a scale that moves Illumina research volume.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL34295[ACCN]+AND+gse[ETYP]+AND+2026/04/01:2026/09/30[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GEO","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Quarterly AVITI share of 6000+X is new."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"Published human sequencing sample counts on the two main Illumina HT platforms rose about 4% q/q in Q2'26 and 23% in Q3'26, so research-side activity is not fading.","detail":"6000+X combined human GSMs: 29,303 (Q1'25), 31,946, 32,464, 33,385, 33,780 (Q1'26), 33,890, 41,580 (Q3'26). This is +15.0% y/y for Q1'26 and +30% y/y for Q3'26 vs Q3'25. This works against the thesis that research volumes are muted, though GEO release timing lags and counts samples not Gb.","url":"https://eutils.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/entrez/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=gds&term=GPL24676[ACCN]+AND+gsm[ETYP]+AND+2026/01/01:2026/03/31[PDAT]&retmax=0","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"NCBI GEO","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Combined 6000+X series and y/y growth computation are new."}
